  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Financing Quality Education for All shows clear early scholarly uptake and strong evidence of active use. The book has been cited 6 times in OpenAlex, and it has also accumulated 1,410 OAPEN downloads, suggesting sustained interest from readers working on education finance, equity, and school governance.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2019, this edited scholarly book addresses a policy question with wide relevance: how to finance education systems in ways that balance efficiency, equity, and quality. Its subject profile and the citing literature place it at the intersection of education policy, public finance, political science, and economics, with particular resonance for compulsory and special education.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record is still modest, but it is meaningful. The book has been cited across a small set of later works that engage topics such as school choice, privatization, open government in education, and the effects of additional resources on school outcomes. That pattern suggests the book is being used as part of ongoing debates about educational quasi-markets, resource allocation, and the governance of schooling rather than only as a general background reference.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage evidence is stronger than the citation count alone might suggest. The OAPEN download total indicates that the book has been read extensively in open-access form, which is consistent with its open availability and with interest from an international audience. Library subject classifications also point to relevance for educational policy, student finance, inclusive education, and elementary education, supporting its circulation in teaching and policy-oriented collections.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The funding record shows support from KU Leuven, the Government of the German-speaking Community of Belgium, and Steunpunt Onderwijsonderzoek (SONO) of the Flemish Ministry of Education, aligning the book with institutional and public-interest research on education systems. Overall, the record shows a book that is already being used and cited in a focused scholarly conversation about how to finance quality education, with impact most visible in education research and adjacent policy studies.</p>",
